docs/cli.rst:9097: 141 chars: The ``sqlite-utils memory`` command works similar to ``sqlite-utils query``, but allows you to execute queries against an in-memory database.
docs/cli.rst:9099: 204 chars: You can also pass this command CSV or JSON files which will be loaded into a temporary in-memory table, allowing you to execute SQL against that data without a separate step to first convert it to SQLite.
docs/cli.rst:9111: 146 chars: It takes all of the same output formatting options as :ref:`sqlite-utils query <cli_query>`: ``--csv`` and ``--csv`` and ``--table`` and ``--nl``:
docs/cli.rst:9139: 185 chars: If you have data in CSV or JSON format you can load it into an in-memory SQLite database and run queries against it directly in a single command using ``sqlite-utils memory`` like this:
docs/cli.rst:9152: 208 chars: If your data is JSON it should be the same format supported by the :ref:`sqlite-utils insert command <cli_inserting_data>` - so either a single JSON object (treated as a single row) or a list of JSON objects.
